%I A402196 #13 Nov 27 2026 13:46:56
%S A402196 11,4523,4549,4583,4591,4657,4663,4673,4691,4729,4733,4787,94331,
%T A402196 94379,94397,94399,94421,94439,94463,94513,94529,94531,94541,94543,
%U A402196 94559,94573,95723,95731,95783,95789,95791,95801,95803,95813,97117,97187,97259,97367,97429,97453
%N A402196 Primes p for which pi_{24;13,23}(p) - pi_{24;1,11}(p) = -1, where pi_{m;a,b,...}(x) is the number of primes <= x which are congruent to a or b or ... (mod m).
%C A402196 This sequence is about one of the 21 prime races modulo 24 between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see A401958 for more details. Specifically, here we have H = {1,11} and S = {13,23}, corresponding to T = {chi_{-8}, chi_{-24}}, where chi_d = (d/.) is the Kronecker symbol viewed as a character modulo N = 24.
%C A402196 This sequence gives primes p such that Sum_{primes q<=p} (chi_{-8}(q) + chi_{-24}(q))/2 = 1.
%C A402196 Assuming GRH and LI (see A401958), the set of x with pi_{24;13,23}(x) > pi_{24;1,11}(x) has logarithmic density 0.97338, making the race between {1,11} and {13,23} the 19th most biased among the 21 races between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see the Zenodo preprint "Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24" below.
%C A402196 Let D(x) = pi_{24;13,23}(x) - pi_{24;1,11}(x). Below x0 = 8*10^12 there are respectively 34876145777, 34876274835, 34876248066, 34876268167, 34876282644, 34876258940, 34876298151, and 34876294229 primes congruent to 1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, or 23 modulo 24, giving D(x0) = 162929. There are 7 sign-changing zones giving a total of 416224 terms below x0:
%C A402196   (1) a(1) = 11;
%C A402196   (2) a(2..12) = 4523-4787 (there are 14 primes a(2) <= p <= a(12) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -2 at p = 4547);
%C A402196   (3) a(13..90) = 94331-99103 (there are 244 primes a(13) <= p <= a(90) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -8 at p = 96457);
%C A402196   (4) a(91..112) = 120011-123853 (there are 105 primes a(91) <= p <= a(112) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -9 at p = 123217);
%C A402196   (5) a(113..443) = 3311603-3736927 (there are 23027 primes a(113) <= p <= a(443) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -53 at p = 3401017);
%C A402196   (6) a(444..14893) = 1088025577-1329406303 (there are 7428492 primes a(444) <= p <= a(14893) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -1084 at p = 1262017513);
%C A402196   (7) a(14894..416224) = 815540876651-1143677485109 (there are 9373832817 primes a(14894) <= p <= a(416224) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -22669 at p = 1075298786363).
%C A402196 The total number of primes p <= x0 with D(p) < 0 is 9381284700.
%H A402196 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A402196/b402196.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..14893</a> (the first 6 sign-changing zones)
%H A402196 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24020025">Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A402196 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A402193/a402193_1.txt">Sign-changing zones of five prime number races modulo 24</a>.
%H A402196 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24159887">Sign-changing zones of prime number races between cosets modulo 24, and of pi(x;8,5) - pi(x;8,1), up to 7*10^12</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A402196 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A402196/a402196.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..416224</a> (all terms below 8*10^12).
%H A402196 Greg Martin and Daniel Fiorilli, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Chennai-31Aug10.pdf">Prime number races: An asymptotic formula for the densities</a>, Analytic and Combinatorial Number Theory, Institute of Mathematical Sciences, Chennai, India. August 31, 2010.
%H A402196 Greg Martin, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Canberra-29Nov18.pdf">Prime number races</a>, Pure Math Seminar, UNSW Canberra. November 29, 2018.
%H A402196 Andrey S. Shchebetov, <a href="https://math101.guru/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/01-A3-Presentation-v7.3EN-no.pdf">Testing Chebyshev's bias for prime numbers up to 10^15</a>, EUCYS 2018.
%H A402196 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev%27s_bias">Chebyshev's bias</a>.
%o A402196 (PARI) my(k=0); forprime(p=2, 1e11, if(p%24==1||p%24==11, k--); if(p%24==13||p%24==23, k++); if(k==-1, print1(p, ", ")))
%Y A402196 See A401958 for related sequences.
%K A402196 nonn,new
%O A402196 1,1
%A A402196 _Leandro Hector Gallego_ and _Jianing Song_, Nov 15 2026
